onger and if  the ground starts to dry out that water  vapor would normally help to cool the  air but that's gone and that does  magnify the heat yeah i hear this a lot  from farmers that spells the weather are  now lasting for lasting for weeks rather  than days well while you and i are stuck  in the studio some of our fellow  correspondents have been out feeling the  heat first hand and our climate  correspondent hannah thomas peter has  been in portugal  this has been an absolutely punishing  heat wave for portugal and perhaps most  obvious of course in the wildfires  ripping through the north and center of  the country and now in the south too  climate change is driving the conditions  that are making those experiences worse  for the people who live in those  communities  underneath it all you've got this  incredible heat this prolonged period of  heat which is making it very difficult  for firefighters to battle those fires  but it's also incredibly dry  around 96  of this country is under drought  conditions and what that means is that  the forests in those hills where the  wildfires have been doing the most  damage  our tinder dry acting as kindling  helping these fires really to relight as  soon as the firefighters get one blaze  under control  another pops up fire season in portugal  has extended over the years and local  residents told me that it's definitely  getting worse  so what now  climate change is impacting portugal and  it's really the heat they are worried  about because it's  not just dangerous in terms of wildfires  and public health impacts heat's also  really expensive because there's the  cost of the emergency response but also  people are way less productive in  significant heat events understandably  so  so i interviewed a senior politician the  secretary of state for home affairs  patricia gaspar  who told me  that the country has to shift its  mindset to accept a new reality and to  think really radically about altering  the way this country lives its life  particularly in the high summer months  she was talking about making sure that  big events don't happen in forested  rural areas  she was discussing the possibility of  thinking about altering co-working hours  in the hot summer months to help people  avoid the hottest part of the day  these are big shifts that mimic what  cities and communities in the global  south have been doing for a long time  finding ways to work around  the impact of heat  and the chief climate scientist at the  country's national weather service said  that this country must adapt because the  forecasts in climate science are getting  worse and his main concern is that  there's going to be a smaller and  smaller gap between these extreme events  and unless there's adaptation it's going  to be harder and harder for this country  to recover  as hannah said climate change is  beginning to change the pattern of  people's lives in portugal a country  which like us could once rely on the  neighboring atlantic ocean to shield  them from extreme heat  over in germany they're experiencing  drought right now with the rhine running  low ironically on the anniversary of  terrible floods which killed more than  180 people our europe correspondent  siobhan robbins takes up the story from  a wine cellar  so you join me in a wine cellar and i'm  down here because this is in the r  valley one of the areas which was  devastated last year in the catastrophic  floods that germany experienced and i've  come down because vineyards and wine  were one of the industries badly  affected and we can speak to peter now  and peter just tell me how bad was the  flooding here it was a total catastrophe  inside of this cellar completely flooded  i see you maybe you can i will show you  this on the wall so the water stands  between the number two and number three  and we are lost by night here in this  cellar in the tanks and barrets 40 000  liters of wine and also 20 000 bottles  inside are completely destroyed by night  wow and and you've got these bottles  here if you just show us something these  are bottles that were  impacted in the flood covered in mud and  i understand you're now  auctioning them off now yes so we are  also looking in the future and we will  be innovated and uh so we make an nft as  this once and there's auction also and  we are looking for this one what's  happening with these flood wines these  are completely everyone is flood wine  and every bottle looks different like  the other ones and it's a kind of art  also in a kind of remember at the  catastrophe and all the money here will  go to help the region rebuild  it's a difficult time for germany though  isn't it because the war in ukraine has  put severe pressure on your energy yet  at the same time all of us here in  europe are living through changing  climate there's heat waves there's  floods  for you how difficult is it is does  climate still take a lead or are you  more focused on getting energy  so uh for us here in this cellar and the  wineries so let's not be implanted we  are also changes we are changes to wood  power and restarting completely because  everything was destroyed here the  heaters was destroyed everything and so  and yeah in time we don't need gas  inside this this cellar peter thank you  and it's an interesting  tug of war here in germany like many  places around the world because of the  pressure on energy some people are  questioning is climate still as  important should it take a back foot  should we be using coal at the moment to  plug the gap at the moment the majority  of public is still saying that climate  is important  but we're seeing every single day  the impact of climate change on our  lives  and thomas i gather you've been out tree  hugging in guildford looking at  something that isn't necessarily a  solution but a cool adaptation to our  sweltering cities yes very much so trees  are nature's air conditioners and they  are that low-tech solution for 