bsite or the sky news app  well later we'll be answering some of  your questions i love this question from  earl who asked me on youtube  my electricity bill is supposed to be  100 renewable and the production cost of  those renewables has been going down  so how can they blame rising gas prices  for my bill going up well your answer  when we come back  [Music]  welcome back to the climate show now  before the break erl asked us why his  bills are still going up despite having  a renewables tariff when it's fossil  fuel prices that are rocketing well for  an answer let's hear from our business  correspondent helen ann smith  hi all thanks for that question uh the  key reason for that is it's not your  energy provider that distributes your  energy that still comes  from the national grid and crucially  it's physically impossible the way the  national grid is set up for any one  particular house to be supplied with  just one energy source uh ofgem like to  use the analogy of a smoothie uh so this  the smoothie of different energy sources  still supplies each house and the more  people who buy renewable tariffs the  more that the renewable part of that  smoothie would go up but there is still  natural gas in their  nuclear and other things  and of course that means that your  tariff is still exposed to the gas price  in that way the other reason is because  of the way that companies purchase  renewable energy so when you sign up to  your tariff uh your renewable tariff  your energy company has likely uh  possibly invested in renewable energy uh  to about the same kilowatts that it  would take to power your house that's  kind of how the trade-off works and  unfortunately the cost of that  investment is also pretty exposed uh to  the energy market more broadly so the  cost of your company paying for that  renewable energy has likely also gone up  because of the gas price going up and  that unfortunately uh gets passed on to  you i hope that goes some way to  answering your question  a revelation there that perhaps those  100 renewable tariffs aren't always  quite what they seem  now as you may know by now we love a  climate change solution on this show  many of them high tech like last week's  one involving graphene so a drum roll  please for today's contender  [Music]  a rock basil a rock with some famous  outcrops this is stafford with its  distinctive basalt columns and this is  my effort at an album cover there with  my boys but that's enough holiday snaps  it's time for your two favorite lessons  combined geography and chemistry  basalt has been capturing carbon for  millennia in a process known as  weathering  carbon dioxide mixes with rainwater in  the air to form a weak acid and when  that falls on rocks like basalt it  reacts  combining co2 with chemicals in the rock  to make carbonate and bicarbonate  molecules these then wash off the  mountain into the soil where it helps to  make them more fertile and into the  ocean where it makes seawater less  acidic  wherever it ends up the carbon is  captured for millions of years and now  when co2 removal is urgent we've  discovered a way to accelerate the  process called enhanced weathering  a basalt is found in pockets across much  of the north and west of the country  including  this quarry in fife  basalt is a foundation stone of our  economy 39 million tons are dug from  britain each year used in everything  from roads to aircraft engines  here big boulders are shifted then  crushed into smaller chunks ready for  different customers  but there are leftovers that carbon  capture company undo have their eye on  so this is an aggregate which is  produced to make concrete with so  concrete goes into building foundations  and building constructions throughout  the united kingdom  over this side we have a 14 to 6 mil  product which is used to make asphalt so  that goes into tarmac to make roads with  the by-product the material here  is what we're interested in this is the  undo fine granule material and as you  can see it comes out  in enormous quantities until you got  here this would have been a waste  product yes it was yes and now we're  fine to be used for it  that use is getting paid in carbon  credits for shifting it and spreading it  on farmland  what are you digging out to show me that  i'm wanting to show you the particle  size the increased surface area in in  this material is where we're wanting it  to hit and capture the carbon what do  you think when you see a huge pile like  this the potential of it what do i think  i think win win win never mind building  another rocket to the moon it's here  it's around us it's under our feet and  we should be getting on with it  jennifer's excitement is shared by many  scientists a recent report from  sheffield university said spreading rock  dust would be a huge step to meeting our  net zero targets and used globally could  absorb five percent of the world's co2  emissions  when we're using this dust  we are capturing the carbon for hundreds  of thousands of years this is a simple  way right under our feet um that we  should be realizing now we don't have to  build massive plants and we don't have  to build a massive infrastructure it's  here it's nature's energy and we should  be using it at the moment you've got  quarrying transport and spreading all of  which use fossil fuels that's got to  undermine this idea a little bit hasn't  it we do have to take that in account so  we know we're capturing x amount of  carbon we have to subtract five percent  of that um  for the trucking operation we've got 95  we've captured it but keeping that  figure low means loads only go to farms  within 50 miles of the quarry and this  load of dust has come about 40 miles  to farm here  it's then spread across 450 acres where  alongside helping the climate the  minerals help the crops too  what's going to be growing out of this  field in a few months uh winter wheat  will follow this not that there's  much to see at the moment what are you  hoping spreading this stuff will do for  the crop that grows here  well hopefully and  improve its health  therefore we won't have to use as many  fungicides fertilizers and what about  the climate benefit does that mean  anything to you as well that's of  interest to me but that's secondary to  me growing my crop  a waste material that could fight  climate change and grow more food no  wonder it's being nicknamed magic dust  well who would have thought that the  simple elements of water  and rock could make such a difference to  our climate  sorry let's almost have to clean that up  later but we do still have time to  answer a couple of the questions that  you've been sending in  and james asked via our app whenever  temperature is mentioned it's always  hottest since records began when did  records begin given the earth is over 4  billion years old what were temperatures  like a thousand years ago 10 000 years  ago or 100 000 years ago  well james the oldest continuous  temperature record began in 1659 it's  called the central england temperature  data series but there's not really  enough data before the year 1880 for  scientists to estimate the average  temperature for the planet  for the thousands of years before then  scientists use proxy measures like these  ice cores drilling into old ice and  analyzing trapped air bubbles for  evidence of past weather the oldest  continuous ice cores in antarctica  stretch back 800 000 years and the rise  in temperature corresponds to a rise in  co2 in the atmosphere  and now a question from rishi and  presumably not that one what measures  can be put in place in the summer to  prevent people from getting seriously  ill or dying and will new uk houses have  to be built differently to be cooler  radhika kosler is one of the world's  leading experts on cooling  key ways to adapt to extreme heat one  make sure that everyone stays hydrated  and stays out of the sun  second to make sure that when cooling  appliances are purchased whether they  are fans or whether they are air  conditioners to make sure that they are  at the highest level of energy  efficiency this is really important to  make sure that the forms of cooling that  are used are sustainable the third is to  focus on the building stock we really  can't get away from buildings and the  built environment as being a key  solution to this extreme heat so making  sure that our buildings are well  insulated that they provide for shading  that they provide for ventilation and  then fourth at the city level to make  sure that we have enough spaces such as  green spaces such as water bodies  such as shaded regions that provide  relief from the extreme heat for those  that are outdoors  and if you want your questions answered  you can scan the qr code here or go to  any of our social media accounts where  you can add questions in the comments  and of course we can look at some of  your questions in more detail victoria  taylor asked us about the impact that  air travel has on climate change now  that is a very good question and one  that we'll be looking at in greater  detail  next week and just a little bit of good  news before we go a massive offshore  development has been just announced by  germany which not only will have huge  turbines in the sea but it will use some  of the power to create hydrogen from  seawater a process called electrolysis  which is a really big hope for a lot of  renewable technology that's it for this  week do stay cool in a very very hot  week to come and look forward to seeing  you again next weekend  [Music]

